        "id": "L3-1-2",
        "title": "Authentication of digital and physical exhibits",
        "authority": "Federal Rules of Evidence 901–902, 104(b)",
        "scenario": "A party offers a screenshot of a message purportedly sent by a manager, but the phone was shared and its metadata is missing.",
        "reading": "Authentication requires enough evidence for a reasonable factfinder to determine that an item is what its proponent claims. Under Rule 901(b), identifying a distinctive feature, witness knowledge, or a reliable process may support a foundation; no single method is mandatory. Rule 902 lists self-authenticating categories, but that designation does not by itself resolve hearsay, relevance, or reliability disputes. Rule 104(b) governs preliminary issues dependent on a fact. Digital evidence requires attention to account access, chain of custody, alteration, timestamps, device ownership, and corroborating records. A defect in chain of custody may affect weight rather than automatic admissibility depending on its severity and context. In the written analysis, identify the applicable jurisdiction and operative date, state each rule in your own words, apply every material fact, separate strongest competing arguments, explain what evidence is missing, and identify the primary authority that could change the conclusion. A conclusion without those steps is not a complete legal analysis. This instructional draft is not legal advice, an automatic grade, or proof of current-law accuracy.\n\nAPPLIED CASE STUDY — SCREENSHOT AUTHENTICATION. Start with what the screenshot purportedly proves: authorship by the manager, content of a message, date sent, or merely that an image existed on a device. Rule 901(a) requires enough evidence to support a finding that the item is what its proponent claims. A manager's displayed name is not conclusive proof of authorship, especially on a shared phone. Request device access records, message export history, recipient testimony, contemporaneous replies, cloud backup details, and any original file containing metadata. Compare Rule 901(b)(1) witness knowledge and 901(b)(4) distinctive characteristics; examine whether self-authentication under Rule 902 is actually established for the specific electronic record. For a challenge, construct three competing explanations: the manager sent the message, another phone user did, or a screenshot was altered. Explain which additional evidence makes each explanation more or less probable. After authentication, analyze hearsay separately: a manager's statement might qualify as an opposing-party statement in some circumstances, but an employee's title does not establish Rule 801(d)(2)(D) scope by itself. Finally, determine whether a missing original raises a genuine best-evidence issue and whether an accurate duplicate is permissible under Rules 1001–1003. Exercise: draft a twelve-question foundation examination, six cross-examination questions, an objection, and a short proposed ruling, noting every material uncertainty.",
        "counterarguments": "Proponent relies on distinctive wording and corroborating payment records. Opponent challenges shared access and incomplete logs, and seeks testimony from the device custodian.",
