        "scenario": "An engineer bases an opinion on four selectively sampled devices.",
        "reading": "Under the amended Federal Rule of Evidence 702 (effective December 1, 2023), the proponent must demonstrate to the court that it is more likely than not that the expert testimony satisfies the rule's admissibility requirements, including helpfulness, sufficient facts or data, reliable principles and methods, and an opinion reflecting reliable application to case facts. The judge decides this under Rule 104(a); not every weakness is automatically only a question of weight. Distinguish the court's admissibility gatekeeping role from the jury's assessment of weight. Evaluate selection bias, validation, error and fit to the actual dispute. The student must specify procedural posture, missing facts and controlling-law uncertainties, then provide an element-by-element analysis of the competing positions. Avoid treating a contested legal outcome as certain. Verify authority for the relevant jurisdiction and decision date.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: An engineer bases an opinion on four selectively sampled devices. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Proponent cites experience; opponent identifies methodological gaps. Identify the exact proposition supported by FRE 702 and Daubert, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
