        "reading": "Attorney-client privilege protects qualifying confidential legal communications, not every communication involving a lawyer. Identify client, counsel role, legal advice purpose, confidentiality, third-party presence and waiver. Work-product protection is distinct and may attach to documents prepared in anticipation of litigation. Rule 502 addresses some effects of disclosure. Do not assume corporate investigations are all privileged or that naming a document confidential creates privilege. Worked method: identify the procedural posture, list the rule elements, write a fact-supported argument for each side, evaluate missing evidence, and explain the possible outcomes without claiming that jurisdictional questions are settled. Confirm the controlling versions of primary authorities before using this work in practice.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: Corporate counsel interviews employees following a product recall; notes are circulated to public-relations consultants. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Company claims legal advice and work product; claimant asserts business purpose and disclosure outside a protected relationship. Identify the exact proposition supported by Federal Rules of Evidence 501 and 502; governing common law, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
