        "id": "L3-1-8",
        "title": "Judicial notice and evidentiary presumptions",
        "authority": "Federal Rules of Evidence 201 and 301",
        "scenario": "At trial, one party asks a judge to treat a disputed internet article as an indisputable fact and invokes a statutory presumption concerning mailed notice.",
        "reading": "Judicial notice of an adjudicative fact requires that it cannot reasonably be questioned, either because it is generally known in the territory or accurately determined from sources whose accuracy cannot reasonably be questioned. A website's existence does not make its factual assertions indisputable. Rule 301 generally shifts the production burden for a civil presumption without shifting persuasion unless another rule governs. Distinguish admissibility, judicial notice, and evidentiary burdens. In criminal trials a noticed fact cannot be forced on the jury as conclusive. To work this problem, identify the relevant forum and controlling date, diagram the required doctrinal elements, list specific supporting and adverse facts, and examine at least two changes to the facts. Distinguish what a cited primary authority actually holds from assumptions about its reach. Treat statutory changes and later decisions as necessary research checks, not matters of guesswork.\n\nPractical evidence workshop. Counsel offers a city weather archive to prove that it rained at 3 p.m., and a blog post to prove the defendant was warned about a defect. Decide separately whether the court may notice the archive's accuracy, the existence of a post, or the truth of the post's accusation. Rule 201 concerns adjudicative facts, not a shortcut around hearsay, authentication, or disputed inferences. A party requesting notice must supply appropriate information, and the opponent may request an opportunity to be heard. Draft distinct jury instructions: in a civil case the jury must accept a properly noticed adjudicative fact as conclusive; in a criminal case it may, but need not, accept it. Under Rule 301, an ordinary federal civil presumption shifts the burden of producing rebuttal evidence, not the ultimate burden of persuasion, unless another federal statute or rule governs. Map what evidence triggers the mailed-notice presumption and what rebuttal evidence defeats the production inference. Research: Federal Rules of Evidence 201 and 301, https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_201 and https://www.law.cornell.edu/rules/fre/rule_301. Verify current versions before submission.",
