        "reading": "Identify each party's substantive burden and the allocation of preliminary admissibility findings. Distinguish sufficiency of proof, admissibility, preservation of objections and harmless error. Explain that appellate courts apply different standards to legal conclusions, factual findings and discretionary evidentiary decisions. Construct an exhibit-by-exhibit table showing objection, response, ruling, preservation and claimed prejudice. Worked process: (1) identify which facts are stipulated and which need proof; (2) list precise statutory or doctrinal elements; (3) present the proponent's reasoning; (4) construct the strongest opposing theory; (5) explain the required evidentiary or procedural decision; (6) verify jurisdiction and time-specific controlling law. A conclusory response is inadequate.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: At trial the court excludes a technical witness but admits disputed documentary evidence; the verdict turns on the distinction between burden of production and burden of persuasion. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: One party argues the governing threshold and evidence justify immediate relief; the opponent contests the legal standard, characterization of disputed facts and available remedy. Provide both positions without inventing record facts. Identify the exact proposition supported by Trial court allocation of proof, FRE 104, 301, 403; applicable appellate standards, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
