        "reading": "Identify formation, partner authority, fiduciary loyalty, disclosure, property rights, accounting and dissolution triggers under the applicable partnership statute and agreement. Distinguish wrongful dissociation from ordinary withdrawal and avoid assuming any partner owns particular partnership assets individually. Track transactions, benefits diverted and possible remedies. Worked analysis sequence: specify jurisdiction and time, identify the contested element and its burden, list facts supporting and undermining each position, then state a conditional conclusion. Explain how a change in one fact would alter the result. Primary law must be independently checked for currency, subsequent treatment and applicability.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: Three partners disagree after one secretly diverts a longstanding partnership customer to a new venture. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Departing partner argues customer followed voluntarily; remaining partners cite confidential records and pre-departure solicitation. Identify the exact proposition supported by Uniform Partnership Act variants and governing state statutes, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
