        "id": "L3-2-8",
        "title": "Corporate veil piercing and entity separateness",
        "authority": "Governing state corporate and LLC statutes and controlling decisions",
        "scenario": "An owner commingles company funds, signs customer contracts under the entity's name and transfers its assets before a large judgment.",
        "reading": "Limited liability is a starting rule, not an unqualified shield for misconduct. Veil-piercing standards differ across jurisdictions and entity types; examine ownership, control, recordkeeping, undercapitalization, misuse and the injustice alleged. Mere sole ownership or business failure is not automatically enough. Distinguish direct personal tort liability, contractual guaranties, successor liability and fraudulent-transfer remedies from veil piercing. To work this problem, identify the relevant forum and controlling date, diagram the required doctrinal elements, list specific supporting and adverse facts, and examine at least two changes to the facts. Distinguish what a cited primary authority actually holds from assumptions about its reach. Treat statutory changes and later decisions as necessary research checks, not matters of guesswork.\n\nPractical litigation workshop. Build a ledger showing transfers among the owner's personal account, the operating LLC, and a related holding company during the six months before judgment. Test three distinct causes of action: (a) the owner's direct liability for the owner's own misrepresentation, (b) a state fraudulent-transfer or voidable-transaction remedy against an asset recipient, and (c) veil piercing against an alter ego. These have different elements and defendants. In applying Delaware-oriented veil analysis, examine whether the enterprise functioned as a single economic entity and whether respect for separateness would work fraud or injustice connected to misuse of the form; ordinary nonpayment alone does not automatically satisfy that second concern. Formally separate corporate and LLC structures may be governed by different statutes and controlling decisions. Draft targeted discovery into bank records, equity ledgers, decision authority, insurance, and transaction consideration rather than assuming undercapitalization decides the case. Then switch the forum to a second state and identify what conflicts-of-law issue must be decided before importing Delaware standards. Source trail: relevant formation statutes and state supreme court decisions; compare a federal application at https://www.ded.uscourts.gov/sites/ded/files/opinions/25-797.pdf (not a substitute for controlling state precedent).",
