        "reading": "Define entity rights, voting requirements, fiduciary arrangements, buy-sell provisions and judicial remedies. Some states permit oppression or deadlock remedies while others apply narrower statutory routes. Separate employment rights from ownership and assess contractual exits, court-ordered buyout, receivership or dissolution. Avoid promising a particular remedy without selected state law. Worked process: (1) identify which facts are stipulated and which need proof; (2) list precise statutory or doctrinal elements; (3) present the proponent's reasoning; (4) construct the strongest opposing theory; (5) explain the required evidentiary or procedural decision; (6) verify jurisdiction and time-specific controlling law. A conclusory response is inadequate.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: Two equal shareholders cannot approve financing, one excludes the other from payroll and they dispute dissolution. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: One party argues the governing threshold and evidence justify immediate relief; the opponent contests the legal standard, characterization of disputed facts and available remedy. Provide both positions without inventing record facts. Identify the exact proposition supported by Chosen state's close-corporation statutes and controlling opinions, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
