        "scenario": "A commission subpoenas broad customer records from a licensed firm before charging any violation.",
        "reading": "Identify statutory investigative authority and the permissible purpose, scope and burden of an administrative subpoena. Distinguish document production from physical entry, and administrative processes from criminal warrants. Evaluate relevance, compliance costs, privilege claims, record protection and lawful judicial enforcement. The existence of a subpoena does not automatically resolve every privacy objection. Worked analysis sequence: specify jurisdiction and time, identify the contested element and its burden, list facts supporting and undermining each position, then state a conditional conclusion. Explain how a change in one fact would alter the result. Primary law must be independently checked for currency, subsequent treatment and applicability.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A commission subpoenas broad customer records from a licensed firm before charging any violation. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Agency argues records establish compliance patterns; firm challenges excessive breadth and seeks narrowing. Identify the exact proposition supported by APA, enabling statute, Fourth Amendment administrative-search principles, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
