        "title": "Rulemaking record and reasoned explanation",
        "authority": "5 U.S.C. §706; Motor Vehicle Manufacturers Association v. State Farm, 463 U.S. 29 (1983)",
        "scenario": "A transportation agency reverses a safety requirement without evaluating a major alternative that commenters documented.",
        "reading": "Arbitrary-and-capricious review examines whether the agency considered important aspects of the problem, explained its decision and connected facts to its choice. Courts do not substitute preferred policy merely because they disagree. Policy changes may be permissible but require reasoned acknowledgment and evaluation under governing precedents. Separate review of policy judgment from legal authorization and compliance with APA procedures; identify the contents of the administrative record. To work this problem, identify the relevant forum and controlling date, diagram the required doctrinal elements, list specific supporting and adverse facts, and examine at least two changes to the facts. Distinguish what a cited primary authority actually holds from assumptions about its reach. Treat statutory changes and later decisions as necessary research checks, not matters of guesswork.\n\nPractical administrative-record workshop. Prepare a two-column record chart: agency reasons for abandoning the safety requirement, and significant countervailing evidence in comments concerning injuries, costs, and a less restrictive alternative. Under 5 U.S.C. § 706(2)(A), the judicial question is whether the agency supplied a reasoned explanation and addressed important aspects of the problem, not whether a judge prefers the old rule. State Farm is central to unexplained reversals and failure to consider important alternatives; do not turn it into a rule demanding the same policy forever. Distinguish a genuinely new factual finding from a change in policy judgment; identify which of the agency's explanations actually appeared in the administrative record instead of inventing post hoc litigation rationales. Separately address standing, final agency action, the applicable procedural route, prejudicial error, and the requested disposition. Draft both a petitioner's proposed argument for remand and an agency response identifying record citations for its choice. Source: 5 U.S.C. § 706, https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/706; verify State Farm and later governing precedent before citation.",
