        "title": "Judicial remedies against regulators",
        "authority": "APA 5 U.S.C. §§ 702–706 and applicable equity and jurisdiction statutes",
        "scenario": "A manufacturer wins a challenge to an unlawful rule but disputes whether the agency must refund paid charges.",
        "reading": "Separate vacatur, remand, injunction and money relief, examining sovereign immunity and the scope of statutory authorization. Winning on legal interpretation does not automatically establish a damages remedy. Review standing, mootness, final agency action, remedial discretion and governing precedent. Consider how a remand without vacatur or prospective relief would operate. Worked process: (1) identify which facts are stipulated and which need proof; (2) list precise statutory or doctrinal elements; (3) present the proponent's reasoning; (4) construct the strongest opposing theory; (5) explain the required evidentiary or procedural decision; (6) verify jurisdiction and time-specific controlling law. A conclusory response is inadequate.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A manufacturer wins a challenge to an unlawful rule but disputes whether the agency must refund paid charges. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: One party argues the governing threshold and evidence justify immediate relief; the opponent contests the legal standard, characterization of disputed facts and available remedy. Provide both positions without inventing record facts. Identify the exact proposition supported by APA 5 U.S.C. §§ 702–706 and applicable equity and jurisdiction statutes, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
