        "scenario": "A law firm sends direct messages promising guaranteed multimillion-dollar verdicts to people identified through recent accident reports.",
        "reading": "Lawyer communications cannot be materially false or misleading under governing professional rules. Solicitation restrictions depend on medium, purpose, recipient and constitutional limits; jurisdictional rules vary. Identify factual support for claimed results, required disclosures, privacy risks and vulnerable recipients. A disclaimer does not necessarily cure a misleading guarantee. Distinguish permissible public education from targeted solicitation and improper contact. To work this problem, identify the relevant forum and controlling date, diagram the required doctrinal elements, list specific supporting and adverse facts, and examine at least two changes to the facts. Distinguish what a cited primary authority actually holds from assumptions about its reach. Treat statutory changes and later decisions as necessary research checks, not matters of guesswork.\n\nPractical ethics intake lab. Classify the firm's messages as ordinary public advertising, targeted written solicitation, or live person-to-person contact; the category matters. ABA Model Rule 7.1 prohibits false or misleading communications about legal services. Under the ABA's Model Rule 7.3, live person-to-person solicitation motivated significantly by financial gain is restricted, subject to stated exceptions; even otherwise permissible solicitation cannot persist after a recipient declines or involve coercion, duress, or harassment. The ABA Model Rules are not themselves the binding ethics code in every state. Compare the ABA's approach to a jurisdiction such as California, which has its own live-contact and written advertising requirements. The promise of a 'guaranteed' multimillion-dollar recovery should be analyzed for objective support, implied unjustified expectations, and possible required labels. Write a revised compliant client-contact script, a five-question conflicts and confidentiality intake checklist, and a supervisor's audit protocol for automated outreach. Sources: https://www.americanbar.org/groups/professional_responsibility/publications/model_rules_of_professional_conduct/rule_7_3_direct_contact_with_prospective_clients/ and https://www.calbar.ca.gov/legal-professionals/rules/rules-professional-conduct/current-rules-professional-conduct/chapter-7-information-about-legal-services.",
