        "scenario": "A lawyer misses a limitations deadline and fails to tell the client, who later rejects a settlement based on incomplete advice.",
        "reading": "Distinguish professional discipline, malpractice liability and breach of fiduciary duty. Analyze duty, breach, causation including the underlying case, damages, conflicts and communication obligations. An ethics rule violation does not always create an independent private cause of action. Form a corrective plan, consider insurance and professional obligations without suggesting concealment. Worked process: (1) identify which facts are stipulated and which need proof; (2) list precise statutory or doctrinal elements; (3) present the proponent's reasoning; (4) construct the strongest opposing theory; (5) explain the required evidentiary or procedural decision; (6) verify jurisdiction and time-specific controlling law. A conclusory response is inadequate.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A lawyer misses a limitations deadline and fails to tell the client, who later rejects a settlement based on incomplete advice. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: One party argues the governing threshold and evidence justify immediate relief; the opponent contests the legal standard, characterization of disputed facts and available remedy. Provide both positions without inventing record facts. Identify the exact proposition supported by Applicable jurisdiction malpractice law, adopted rules and professional standards, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
        "counterarguments": "One party argues the governing threshold and evidence justify immediate relief; the opponent contests the legal standard, characterization of disputed facts and available remedy. Provide both positions without inventing record facts.",
        "assignment": "Submit a 1,300–1,700-word research memorandum, annotated timeline, fact-to-element grid and competing rulings. Use at least three independently verified primary authorities. Human review rubric: governing law 25, application 30, counterargument 15, citation validity 20, written organization 10.",
