        "id": "L3-4-10",
        "title": "Professional Responsibility — Second-Year Final Examination",
        "authority": "All primary authorities taught in this course; verify the applicable jurisdiction and law as of the assessment date.",
        "scenario": "FICTIONAL FINAL CASE FILE: A law firm jointly represents a startup and its CEO. During an internal interview, the CEO discloses possible false statements in a filed affidavit; a former client asks the firm to oppose the startup in an affiliated matter. The firm used an automated legal draft containing fictitious citations. Evaluate representation identity, concurrent/former-client conflicts, confidentiality, candor, supervision, and remediation. Identify missing material facts rather than inventing them. Your role is counsel for both sides in a simulated written examination.",
        "reading": "Examination instructions: begin with a jurisdictional and procedural statement. Make an issue tree connecting at least four previously studied doctrines. For each doctrine, articulate elements and burdens, apply the material facts, test a plausible opposing interpretation and state the strongest supported outcome. Identify controlling authority, distinguish binding from persuasive sources and verify later negative treatment. Give remedies and procedural steps separately. A written examination cannot be passed automatically by answering a multiple-choice question. The grading rubric is: issue identification and rule accuracy 25 points; detailed fact application 30; persuasive counterarguments 15; authentic and current primary citations 20; structure and professional clarity 10. Cite all factual assumptions and use an uncertainty register. Work should be checked by a qualified human reviewer before recording mastery.",
        "counterarguments": "The examiner must challenge the candidate's selected rule, burden, key factual assumption, proposed remedy and reliance on a seemingly analogous precedent. Student must respond to five fact variations and explain why at least one initially attractive answer fails. The examiner must supply at least one fact change affecting a jurisdiction-specific element, and require the student to explain which authority controls.",
        "assignment": "FINAL: Write 2,000–2,500 words comprising a researched legal memorandum, adverse position, decision tree, evidence table and recommendation. Produce a five-minute oral argument outline and a 100-point self-review, then submit for human substantive review. This final remains ungraded until a qualified reviewer approves it.\n\nFINAL EXAMINATION ISSUE-APPLICATION TASKS:\nA. Identify every current and former client and specify whether counsel represents the startup, the CEO personally, or both. Explain the consequences for an internal interview and organizational-client warnings.\nB. Construct a Rule 1.7 and Rule 1.9 conflict table distinguishing material adversity, substantial relationship, informed consent requirements, and potentially nonconsentable conflicts under the applicable state's adopted rules.\nC. Evaluate the CEO's affidavit: who knows it is false, what corrective steps counsel must take, the different duties of confidentiality and candor, and whether an applicable exception or court order permits or demands disclosure. Do not assume every suspicion justifies disclosure.\nD. Investigate the fabricated citations in the legal draft: verification process, supervising lawyer responsibilities, tribunal correction duties, and client communication. Propose immediate remediation, document preservation, and a training change.\nFACT CHANGES: The affidavit was never filed; the former client consented after independent counsel; the false citation was discovered before submission. Explain the distinct consequences and the governing local rules.\nREVIEW STANDARD: The written submission must include controlling primary authorities, adverse arguments, a source/date check, and a fact-change analysis. The knowledge-check quiz is not a substitute for a qualified human review of the full examination.",
