        "reading": "Determine whether property is a capital asset, adjusted basis, relevant holding period and recognition rules. Rate and deduction conclusions depend on the relevant tax year. Distinguish realized gain from currently taxable income. The student must specify procedural posture, missing facts and controlling-law uncertainties, then provide an element-by-element analysis of the competing positions. Avoid treating a contested legal outcome as certain. Verify authority for the relevant jurisdiction and decision date.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: An individual sells shares held nine months and land held several years. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Taxpayer argues both are long-term capital gains; examiner challenges character. Identify the exact proposition supported by Internal Revenue Code sections 1221–1223, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.\n\nAPPLIED CASE STUDY — MIXED CAPITAL AND ORDINARY TRANSACTIONS. Consider a taxpayer who sells nine-month shares, four-year investment land, and equipment used in a business. Create a disposition-by-disposition ledger of cost, basis adjustments, amount realized, recognized gain or loss, holding period, and character. Apply § 1221 exclusions and § 1231 analysis where relevant, then determine whether any depreciation recapture under §§ 1245 or 1250 requires ordinary income or special gain treatment. Do not net every gain into a single 'capital gain' category before classification. If a taxpayer is engaged in securities dealing, examine whether particular holdings are inventory or investment property under governing facts and elections. For comparison, explain the consequences of selling land held primarily for sale to customers in the ordinary course of business rather than for investment. Exercise: draft a classification memorandum supporting both the taxpayer's preferred treatment and a plausible IRS challenge, with primary authority and tax-year assumptions. Include one sensitivity calculation showing how a $6,000 basis adjustment changes gain without changing the underlying asset's legal character.",
