        "reading": "Begin with taxpayer identity, residence, nature of income, source rules, applicable tax year and any relevant treaties. Cross-border taxation requires separating US domestic-law sourcing from treaty allocation, foreign tax credits and possible effectively connected income or permanent establishment concepts. Digital activities complicate factual classification. Do not assume customer location alone fixes tax source or that a treaty applies without eligibility. To work this problem, identify the relevant forum and controlling date, diagram the required doctrinal elements, list specific supporting and adverse facts, and examine at least two changes to the facts. Distinguish what a cited primary authority actually holds from assumptions about its reach. Treat statutory changes and later decisions as necessary research checks, not matters of guesswork.\n\nPractical cross-border classification lab. Construct a table separating customer country, contractual counterparty, employees performing services, intellectual-property owner, server operation, invoicing entity, and the taxpayer's residence. Revenue from a digital subscription cannot be assigned a source solely because the customer or server is abroad. First characterize each payment under the governing tax rules, then analyze its applicable sourcing provision and any statutory exceptions. Contrast a service-fee contract requiring substantial human performance with a license granting rights to exploit intellectual property; the different characterization may change sourcing. Next test whether the taxpayer is a domestic corporation, foreign corporation, or individual, because worldwide-income, effectively-connected-income, withholding, and foreign-tax-credit questions differ. A treaty and a permanent-establishment inquiry must be checked only where relevant, including limitations on benefits and current treaty text. Calculate alternative outcomes using explicitly assumed facts rather than implying a single tax result. Primary research queue: IRC §§ 61, 861–865 and any relevant Code provisions, Treasury regulations, treaty articles, and applicable tax-year amendments. Do not use a general 'server location equals tax residence' shortcut.",
