        "scenario": "A taxpayer receives a proposed deficiency after claiming a deduction, but the assessment period and administrative record are disputed.",
        "reading": "Determine the notice type, relevant tax year, statutory deadline, deficiency procedures and available administrative or judicial forum. Distinguish a proposed adjustment from a statutory notice of deficiency. Verify the correct limitation period and exceptions, and explain why missing an operative filing date can forfeit an option. Use a chronology, not a guessed deadline. Worked process: (1) identify which facts are stipulated and which need proof; (2) list precise statutory or doctrinal elements; (3) present the proponent's reasoning; (4) construct the strongest opposing theory; (5) explain the required evidentiary or procedural decision; (6) verify jurisdiction and time-specific controlling law. A conclusory response is inadequate.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A taxpayer receives a proposed deficiency after claiming a deduction, but the assessment period and administrative record are disputed. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: One party argues the governing threshold and evidence justify immediate relief; the opponent contests the legal standard, characterization of disputed facts and available remedy. Provide both positions without inventing record facts. Identify the exact proposition supported by Internal Revenue Code §§ 6212–6213, 6501, 7803; applicable IRS procedures, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
        "counterarguments": "One party argues the governing threshold and evidence justify immediate relief; the opponent contests the legal standard, characterization of disputed facts and available remedy. Provide both positions without inventing record facts.",
