        "reading": "Evaluate causation, foreseeability, reasonable certainty, mitigation and contractual limitations. Distinguish revenue from net lost profit and avoid double recovery. Explain alternative reliance or restitution theories where available. The student must specify procedural posture, missing facts and controlling-law uncertainties, then provide an element-by-element analysis of the competing positions. Avoid treating a contested legal outcome as certain. Verify authority for the relevant jurisdiction and decision date.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: Delayed supplies halt production and the purchaser seeks forecast lost profits. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Buyer offers historical margins; supplier points to unrelated equipment failure. Identify the exact proposition supported by State remedies and applicable contract law, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.\n\nADDITIONAL PRACTICE — FORECAST VERSUS PROVEN LOSS. Begin with a forecast showing $200,000 of revenue from the delayed order. Identify which units customers actually canceled, which were fulfilled after production resumed, and which sales were diverted to another supplier. Determine contribution margin based on actual avoidable costs, not projected revenue alone. A damages expert should disclose sources, assumptions, methods, uncertainty, and the sensitivity of results to demand. Compare a business with several years of stable sales data against a newly launched product with little track record; certainty requirements may affect persuasive weight and proof, but a new venture is not automatically barred from recovering damages under every governing law. Address contractual limitation-of-liability clauses, foreseeability, and mitigation without assuming all clauses are enforceable. Exercise: build a table of verified inputs versus assumptions and a high-low loss model, then draft proposed findings for claimant and defense. Explain what additional discovery—customer cancellation notices, purchasing history, production schedules, or accounting records—would narrow the range of legally supportable recovery.",
