        "reading": "Integrated advanced legal examination. First identify the parties, forum, operative date and controlling legal authority. Create an issue tree with each required element, mark disputed and missing facts, and determine which side bears the burden. Draft a position for each side before selecting a reasoned outcome. Different doctrines require separate analyses even when a narrative connects them. Do not rely on the multiple-choice result as a grade for independent writing. Source citations must be verified against primary documents and later controlling developments.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A former executive downloads a customer database and joins a competitor. Employer seeks a broad two-year employment ban and $2 million in projected losses. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Employer stresses imminent disclosure; employee says data is public and broad relief prevents earning a livelihood. Identify the exact proposition supported by Fed. R. Civ. P. 65; governing state trade-secret law; Winter v. NRDC, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
