        "reading": "Restitution focuses on unjust retention of benefits under governing law, and is distinct from expectation damages. Identify benefit conferred, valuation, defenses, change of position and interplay with failed contracts or statutory restrictions. Recovery is not always the invoice or the claimant's costs. Consider whether an enforceable agreement precludes the proposed unjust enrichment theory. Worked method: identify the procedural posture, list the rule elements, write a fact-supported argument for each side, evaluate missing evidence, and explain the possible outcomes without claiming that jurisdictional questions are settled. Confirm the controlling versions of primary authorities before using this work in practice.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A contractor improves a property under a contract later held unenforceable, and the owner retains the improvements. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Contractor claims market value of improvements; owner argues poor workmanship and that the benefit was not requested. Identify the exact proposition supported by Applicable state restitution law; Restatement (Third) of Restitution and Unjust Enrichment as secondary authority, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
