        "authority": "28 U.S.C. §§ 2201–2202; Article III and applicable precedent",
        "scenario": "Two competitors dispute whether a license covers a planned product, but neither has yet brought an infringement claim.",
        "reading": "Declaratory relief is a remedy conditioned on a concrete controversy and independent jurisdiction; the Declaratory Judgment Act does not itself supply subject matter jurisdiction. Evaluate adversity, immediacy, practical legal consequences and the court's discretion where authorized. Differentiate advisory legal opinions from justiciable disputes and account for possible alternative state remedies. To work this problem, identify the relevant forum and controlling date, diagram the required doctrinal elements, list specific supporting and adverse facts, and examine at least two changes to the facts. Distinguish what a cited primary authority actually holds from assumptions about its reach. Treat statutory changes and later decisions as necessary research checks, not matters of guesswork.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: Two competitors dispute whether a license covers a planned product, but neither has yet brought an infringement claim. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Licensee seeks certainty before launch; licensor calls the product hypothetical and facts insufficiently fixed. Identify the exact proposition supported by 28 U.S.C. §§ 2201–2202; Article III and applicable precedent, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.\n\nPractical justiciability workshop. The prospective licensee has spent $400,000 tooling up for a planned launch and received a cease-and-desist letter identifying two patent claims; the licensor later calls the letter 'only a discussion.' Build a chronology showing commercial commitment, specific legal threats, negotiation history, and whether a concrete dispute exists now. Under 28 U.S.C. § 2201, declaratory relief requires a case of actual controversy; the statute does not itself confer subject-matter jurisdiction. Explain why a hypothetical worry differs from substantial adversity of sufficient immediacy and reality, and identify an independent jurisdictional basis. Then consider discretionary reasons a federal court might decline relief despite Article III jurisdiction, without treating discretion as a substitute for the threshold. Draft a complaint paragraph alleging jurisdiction and a motion to dismiss for absence of a ripe, concrete dispute; identify what additional evidence would change the result. Compare a prospective copyright-license issue and a patent dispute rather than assuming identical federal jurisdiction rules. Sources: 28 U.S.C. §§ 2201–2202 and controlling case law, including the Supreme Court's MedImmune framework where applicable.",
        "counterarguments": "Licensee seeks certainty before launch; licensor calls the product hypothetical and facts insufficiently fixed.",
        "assignment": "Prepare a 1,200–1,600-word memorandum with an issue tree, authority table, point-by-point application, best opposing argument, two fact variations and recommended disposition. Score guide: law 25, application 30, counterarguments 15, verified sources 20, clarity 10. Written work requires qualified review. Also submit the practical workshop deliverables and a short primary-authority verification log identifying jurisdiction, rule version, and unresolved questions.",
