        "scenario": "A technology company seeks millions of archived communications, while the opponent alleges missing records after preservation notice.",
        "reading": "Scope and proportionality require relevance and attention to importance, access, resources, burden and benefits. Identify preservation triggers, litigation holds, defensible searches, privilege review and sanctions requirements for lost electronically stored information. Rule 37(e) distinguishes prejudice remedies from intent-to-deprive sanctions. Draft a concrete preservation and production protocol. Worked process: (1) identify which facts are stipulated and which need proof; (2) list precise statutory or doctrinal elements; (3) present the proponent's reasoning; (4) construct the strongest opposing theory; (5) explain the required evidentiary or procedural decision; (6) verify jurisdiction and time-specific controlling law. A conclusory response is inadequate.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A technology company seeks millions of archived communications, while the opponent alleges missing records after preservation notice. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: One party argues the governing threshold and evidence justify immediate relief; the opponent contests the legal standard, characterization of disputed facts and available remedy. Provide both positions without inventing record facts. Identify the exact proposition supported by Federal Rules of Civil Procedure 26, 34, 37, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
