        "scenario": "Police search a seized phone's contents following a lawful arrest.",
        "reading": "Distinguish physical seizure from access to digital contents. A search incident to arrest does not ordinarily authorize a full phone search without warrant or applicable exception. Evaluate specificity, exigency, consent and potential suppression separately. The student must specify procedural posture, missing facts and controlling-law uncertainties, then provide an element-by-element analysis of the competing positions. Avoid treating a contested legal outcome as certain. Verify authority for the relevant jurisdiction and decision date.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: Police search a seized phone's contents following a lawful arrest. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Police argue risk of data deletion; defense disputes concrete exigency. Identify the exact proposition supported by Fourth Amendment and Riley v. California, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.\n\nADDITIONAL PRACTICE — PARTICULARITY AND DIGITAL FORENSICS. The mere fact a device was seized during a valid arrest does not turn its stored data into ordinary pocket contents. Document the precise authority for every later forensic step: consent, warrant, or a recognized exception under controlling law. When a warrant is used, examine the nexus between the alleged offense and the categories of records sought, the relevant time period, and the likelihood that responsive files may be distributed among applications. Avoid categorical claims that every broad search term is unconstitutional or that all digital searches require a rigid search protocol; controlling jurisdiction and warrant language matter. Test an officer's opening of a folder labeled 'personal photographs' while seeking encrypted transaction records. Identify facts supporting either reasonable execution or exploratory overreach. Exercise: produce a digital evidence inventory with timestamps, applications, warrant paragraph authorizing access, and suppression arguments. Include a discussion of inevitable discovery or good faith only after independently deciding whether the underlying search violated the Fourth Amendment.",
