        "reading": "A guilty plea generally requires knowing, voluntary and intelligent decision-making. Examine counsel performance, disclosure, lawful bargaining pressure, Rule 11 safeguards and the applicable prejudice standard for ineffective-assistance claims. A severe charging threat is not automatically unconstitutional, but misrepresentation or unlawful coercion may matter. Distinguish direct appeal and post-conviction routes. Worked method: identify the procedural posture, list the rule elements, write a fact-supported argument for each side, evaluate missing evidence, and explain the possible outcomes without claiming that jurisdictional questions are settled. Confirm the controlling versions of primary authorities before using this work in practice.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: A defendant accepts a plea after counsel fails to investigate a surveillance recording and the prosecutor threatens a lawful additional charge. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Defendant argues deficient advice changed the decision; prosecution emphasizes sworn colloquy and strength of admissible evidence. Identify the exact proposition supported by Federal Rules of Criminal Procedure 11 and applicable constitutional cases, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
