        "reading": "Identify the statutory maximum and mandatory minimum, guideline framework and recognized sentencing factors before assessing factfinding. Constitutional rules can restrict which facts raise authorized sentencing ranges, while advisory guideline calculations involve additional complexities. Distinguish direct appeal, preserved objections and collateral review; use controlling law and current guideline editions. Worked analysis sequence: specify jurisdiction and time, identify the contested element and its burden, list facts supporting and undermining each position, then state a conditional conclusion. Explain how a change in one fact would alter the result. Primary law must be independently checked for currency, subsequent treatment and applicability.\n\nCase-specific analytical method: Begin with the actual scenario: Following a guilty verdict, a court relies on contested conduct to impose an unusually severe sentence. Do not assume that the allegations are proven. List each actor, disputed fact, required evidentiary foundation, and burden of proof. Evaluate the strongest response: Prosecution argues credible evidence supports enhancement; defense challenges proof, statutory authority and proportionality. Identify the exact proposition supported by Federal sentencing statutes and guidelines; Sixth and Eighth Amendments, check whether that authority controls in the selected jurisdiction, and distinguish any conflicting or later decisions. Write a provisional conclusion conditioned on facts that remain unknown. Then change one material fact and explain whether the legal outcome would change. This exercise develops legal reasoning; neither a keyword match nor a quiz establishes mastery.",
